{0 JSON}

{!Workero.JSON} reads and writes JSON values of type [Jsonkit.t]. It does not
re-export jsonkit. Add [jsonkit] to your dune [libraries] and [jsonkit.ppx] to
[preprocess] when you derive decoders:

{@dune[
(executable
 (name main)
 (modes wasm)
 (preprocess
  (pps jsonkit.ppx))
 (libraries workero jsonkit))
]}

This handler decodes a request body into a record and returns JSON. The handler
chooses the response for a decode error:

{@ocaml[
open Jsonkit.Primitives

type user = { name : string; age : int } [@@deriving json]

let handler req _env _ctx =
  match Workero.JSON.decode user_of_json req with
  | Ok user -> Workero.JSON.response (user_to_json user)
  | Error message -> Workero.JSON.error message

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.JSON.decode} reads the body once and returns a result. It returns
[Error message] for invalid JSON or when the decoder raises
[Jsonkit.Of_json_error]. It does not itself send a 400 response. An exception
of another kind from the decoder escapes the handler unless you catch it.

Use {!Workero.JSON.parse} to read a [Jsonkit.t] without decoding it. It also
returns a result. {!Workero.JSON.request} instead raises
[Jsonkit.Of_string_error] for invalid JSON. If this exception escapes the
handler, the runtime answers with status 500.

{!Workero.JSON.response} sends compact JSON with status 200 and
[content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8] by default.
{!Workero.JSON.error} sends an [{"error": message}] object with status 400 by
default. You can choose the response status in either function.

Without the ppx, write a decoder with [Jsonkit.Of_json]. See {!Workero.JSON}
for an example and the complete API. See
{{!page-requests}Requests and responses} for other body and response formats.
